taskDescription = "Find the target value in the array using linear search. Check each element sequentially from the beginning until you find the target or reach the end of the array."
taskGuide = "Big O Notation:
- Time Complexity: O(n) in worst and average cases, O(1) best case
- Space Complexity: O(1) - only needs a few variables

Use Cases:
- Small datasets where simplicity matters more than speed
- Unsorted data - works on any arrangement
- When you need to search only once (overhead of sorting not justified)
- Searching for multiple criteria or complex conditions
- Linked lists or data structures without random access

Suitable Data Structures:
- Arrays, lists, linked lists
- Any sequential data structure
- Works on both sorted and unsorted data

Algorithm Steps:

1. Start at the beginning of the array:
   - You'll examine each element one by one
   - No preprocessing or sorting required

2. For each element in sequence:
   - Use check(index) to highlight the current element being examined
   - This shows which position you're currently inspecting

3. Compare the current element with the target:
   - Check if array[index] equals targetValue
   - If it matches, you've found your target

4. If target is found:
   - Use commitSelect(index) to mark this position
   - This highlights the found element and ends the search
   - The search is complete

5. If target is not found:
   - Move to the next element
   - Continue checking until you reach the end
   - If you've checked all elements without finding it, target doesn't exist

6. Early termination:
   - Stop as soon as you find the target
   - No need to check remaining elements
   - Use break to exit the search loop

Key Characteristics:
Simple and intuitive - checks every element until finding the target. Guaranteed to find the element if it exists, but may be slow for large datasets."
exampleCode = "# Linear search
for i in range(len(array)):
    check(i)
    if array[i] == targetValue:
        commitSelect(i)
        break"
